$DIR/main.p:26:2: a pointer to the per-CPU variable ticks is used outside the region it was taken in
    ticks has one copy for each processor, and &ticks points to the copy of the
    processor that ran the code that took it. Here the pointer is used where the
    region it was taken in (preemption or interrupts disabled, or an rt.Lock
    held) is no longer held, so the task may have moved to another processor and
    the pointer reaches that processor's copy while another task or an interrupt
    handler uses it.
    Go has no per-processor variables. A pointer to this processor's copy, used
    after the task has moved, reaches the copy of another processor, which that
    processor's code may be using at the same time.
    Use the pointer only inside the region it was taken in, or take the address
    again where it is needed
    (see "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2023" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:31:2: a pointer to the per-CPU variable ticks is stored where it outlives the region, outside the region it was taken in
    ticks has one copy for each processor, and &ticks points to the copy of the
    processor that runs this code now. The pointer is good only while the task
    cannot move to another processor, so it may be kept only in local variables
    and used only inside the region it was taken in (preemption or interrupts
    disabled, or an rt.Lock held). Here it is stored where it outlives the
    region, where it can be used after the region ends or by another task.
    Go has no per-processor variables. A pointer to this processor's copy, used
    after the task has moved, reaches the copy of another processor, which that
    processor's code may be using at the same time.
    Use the variable by name inside the region, or copy the value out and keep
    the copy:
        rt.DisablePreemption()
        total := ticks
        rt.EnablePreemption()
    (see "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2023" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:39:2: a pointer to the per-CPU variable ticks is passed to remember, which may keep it, outside the region it was taken in
    ticks has one copy for each processor, and &ticks points to the copy of the
    processor that runs this code now. The pointer is good only while the task
    cannot move to another processor, so it may be kept only in local variables
    and used only inside the region it was taken in (preemption or interrupts
    disabled, or an rt.Lock held). Here it is passed to remember, which may keep
    it, where it can be used after the region ends or by another task.
    Go has no per-processor variables. A pointer to this processor's copy, used
    after the task has moved, reaches the copy of another processor, which that
    processor's code may be using at the same time.
    Use the variable by name inside the region, or copy the value out and keep
    the copy:
        rt.DisablePreemption()
        total := ticks
        rt.EnablePreemption()
    (see "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2023" for a longer explanation)
